Authorize the System: Build an ATO Package
Objectives
By completing this lab, you will be able to:

Assemble a structured ATO package (SSP, SAR, POA&M, risk assessment) on Linux
Generate a cryptographically verifiable evidence index
Draft an executive risk summary and AO decision memo
Simulate a formal authorization decision with recorded sign-off
Prerequisites
Working knowledge of NIST RMF / CGRC Domain 6 (Authorize) concepts
Familiarity with Linux CLI, bash scripting, and text-based document formats (Markdown/YAML)
Understanding of SSP, SAR, POA&M artifact purposes
Basic Python 3 scripting ability
Environment Setup
Al Nafi-provided single Linux machine (Ubuntu 22.04+ assumed)
Install required tools:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-pip pandoc tree gnupg jq
pip3 install pyyaml python-docx
Create working directory structure:
mkdir -p ~/ato-package/{ssp,sar,poam,risk-assessment,evidence,briefing,decision}
cd ~/ato-package
Tasks
Task 1: Organize Core ATO Artifacts
Design and populate the four core artifacts as structured Markdown/YAML files. You determine schema and content depth appropriate to a fictitious "example workload" (e.g., a mid-tier web application processing sensitive data).

Requirements:

ssp/system-security-plan.md — system boundary, control implementation summary (pick 8–10 NIST 800-53 controls minimum), data flow
sar/security-assessment-report.md — findings mapped to controls, pass/fail/partial status, risk ratings
poam/poam.csv — weaknesses, milestones, resource estimates, target completion dates (columns: id,weakness,control,severity,status,milestone,due_date)
risk-assessment/risk-assessment.md — threat sources, likelihood/impact matrix, residual risk determination
